WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.950 --> 00:00:04.230
Chris: What is creative problem
solving and what does it consist of?

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:10.200
Possibly the most important skill in creative
problem solving is to be open minded and think

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:15.430
outside of the box as the most obvious reason
for something is not always the solution.

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:21.570
Leah: A creative thinker will look at the
situation from all angles and not just jump to

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:26.959
conclusion or go for the most logical solution,
following the path that has always been followed.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:33.179
They will ask many questions such as ‘why?’ and
‘why not?’ They will look underneath the surface of

00:00:33.179 --> 00:00:35.769
a problem and get to the root cause of the issue.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:41.040
Chris: Once a situation has been thoroughly
explored, the creative thinker will need the

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.410
skills to decide on the best course of action.

00:00:44.019 --> 00:00:49.570
To do this they’ll need to collaborate with others,
share their ideas and brainstorm the best solutions.

00:00:50.469 --> 00:00:55.690
However, unstructured thinking may need to move
to a more structured approach, when looking at how

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:58.350
the solution will be systematically implemented.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:03.330
A creative thinker needs to use a
range of different skills such as

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:04.879
• Leah: Attention to detail

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:06.920
• Good Communication skills

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.180
• Intuitive thinking

00:01:08.599 --> 00:01:11.149
• Systematic thinking and Logical approach

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:16.580
• Looking at things differently, from a fresh
perspective and finding new ways to solve a problem

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:18.370
• Innovative thinking

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.050
• Questioning approach

00:01:20.289 --> 00:01:25.939
• Techniques such as brainstorming, drawing
mind maps and rich pictures, and role-play

00:01:26.539 --> 00:01:28.709
• And always keeping an open mind

00:01:28.849 --> 00:01:34.709
Chris: as well as these soft skills there are key methods
and techniques that can be considered as a framework.

00:01:35.199 --> 00:01:40.199
Anyone can follow these, and they help to ensure
that all the elements of an issue are considered,

00:01:40.649 --> 00:01:46.119
whether it be for a new system, locating an error
or solving a problem such as a customer complaint.

00:01:47.389 --> 00:01:54.509
Leah: Identify problem- It’s critical to ensure that
you understand exactly what the problem is and not just

00:01:54.509 --> 00:01:57.569
rely on information from others as to what they think.

00:01:58.249 --> 00:02:03.889
This might take some time investigating but this is
better than wasting time solving the wrong problem

00:02:03.949 --> 00:02:06.869
only to find that the original one still exists.

00:02:07.469 --> 00:02:12.279
Chris: Clarify problem- To ensure that
your ideas are valid, do your research.

00:02:13.129 --> 00:02:17.469
What are other organisations doing, is
there any data to back up your thinking?

00:02:18.119 --> 00:02:22.639
This should help you to visualise your
ideas more clearly and enable you to sketch

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:25.220
out a draft of how it will be approached.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:31.699
Leah: Use Analytics- The research data that you
gather will help you to see patterns, based on what

00:02:31.769 --> 00:02:35.109
users, competitors and industry bodies are reporting.

00:02:35.709 --> 00:02:41.859
This is particularly useful for new ideas and will
you to understand what might be successful, or not.

00:02:42.589 --> 00:02:47.589
Chris: Use Feedback- These steps will provide you
with enough information to present your ideas to

00:02:47.589 --> 00:02:50.699
others, an important step that shouldn’t be ignored.

00:02:52.019 --> 00:02:56.409
Gaining feedback from colleagues, customers
and other stakeholders can ensure that

00:02:56.409 --> 00:02:59.129
you’ve not overlooked any essential elements.

00:02:59.759 --> 00:03:07.529
Leah: Solve the problem- After all that groundwork is done
you can now finally begin putting your ideas into practice.

00:03:07.939 --> 00:03:12.879
To do this you need to be methodical and not
rush to the end or get distracted along the way.

00:03:13.609 --> 00:03:20.259
Remember to keep the ‘ecosystem’ in mind and do not worry
so much about the finer details and design at this stage.

00:03:20.879 --> 00:03:25.989
You could use ‘Low-fidelity wireframes’ that
outline blueprints for the project layout.

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:32.929
Paper drawn sketches are excellent for this as with
the ecosystem, don’t get bogged down in the detail and

00:03:32.929 --> 00:03:38.989
remember if something’s not working out, to keep an
open mind and explore alternative tools and approaches.

00:03:39.469 --> 00:03:45.629
Chris: And finally, if you get stuck don’t forget go
back to your feedback and listen to comments from your

00:03:45.629 --> 00:03:52.069
colleagues and other stakeholders, remember talking
through an issue can help unblock creative thinking.

